.TH CTHRPOOL_DECLARE_SCOPED 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cthrpool_declare_scoped \- declare a thread pool variable with automatic RAII destruction
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

cthrpool_declare_scoped(name);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
The same as cthrpool_declare(), plus a cleanup attribute, so the pool is
destroyed when the variable leaves scope, exactly as cthrpool_destroy()
would destroy it.

You must still assign the variable before you use it. If it is still
CTHRPOOL_NULL at the end of scope, the cleanup does nothing.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name  Variable name to declare.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
It needs GCC or Clang, because it uses __attribute__((cleanup)).
When the drain behavior matters, run a shutdown yourself before the
end of scope: the shutdown that the destroy runs on its own drains the
queue, and that drain can take any amount of time when the tasks are
long.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
{
    cthrpool_declare_scoped(pool);
    pool = cthrpool_create(4, 0, NULL);
    cthrpool_submit(pool, my_fn, my_arg, NULL);
    cthrpool_shutdown_drain(pool);
}  /* pool is destroyed automatically here */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cthrpool_declare (3),
.BR cthrpool_construct_scoped (3),
.BR cthrpool_destroy (3)
